    "evidence_text": "Thyroid hormone inhibits lung fibrosis in mice by improving epithelial mitochondrial function\n\nDosimetry and risk estimates of radioiodine therapy for large, multinodular goiters.\n\n69 g; 1.9 +/-\n\n0.9 GBq 131I).\n\nAn iodine biokinetic model and the MIRD methodology were used to estimate absorbed doses in organs.\n\nCancer risks were calculated using ICRP Publication 60.   \n\n\n1.4 Gy (thyroid), 1.2 +/-\n\n0.2 Gy (parotid).\n\nAbsorbed doses in the thyroid of 94 +/-\n\n25 Gy for euthyroid and 93 +/-\n\n17 Gy for hyperthyroid patients were calculated (thyroid radioactivity measurements).\n\nA 1.6% life-time risk of development of cancer outside the thyroid gland was calculated.\n\nIn younger patients, surgery may be preferred.\n\nHowever, for elderly patients and patients with cardiopulmonary disease, the advantages of noninvasive radioiodine treatment will outweight the life-time risk of this mode of therapy.\n\nEffects on tumor growth and metastasis, blood perfusion, oxygenation, and gastrointestinal toxicity were studied.\n\nIntetumumab alone had a moderate effect on tumor growth.\n\nRadioiodine treatment of multinodular non-toxic goitre.\n\nOBJECTIVE To investigate the long term effect of radioactive iodine on thyroid function and size in patients with non-toxic multinodular goitre.   \n\n\nPATIENTS 69 patients with a growing multinodular non-toxic goitre causing local compression symptoms or cosmetic inconveniences.\n\nThe treatment was chosen because of a high operative risk, previous thyroidectomy, or refusal to be operated on.   \n\n\nPatients treated with two doses as well as those developing hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism had a significant reduction in thyroid volume.\n\nSide effects were few: three cases of hyperthyroidism and two cases of radiation thyroiditis.\n\nOnly one patient was dissatisfied with the result; she was referred for operation six months after treatment.   \n\n\nIncreased density of tumor-associated macrophages is associated with decreased survival in advanced thyroid cancer.\n\nElectronic medical records were used to gather clinical and pathologic data.\n\nIn conclusion, the density of TAMs is increased in advanced thyroid cancers.\n\nMagnetic resonance imaging for volume estimation of large multinodular goitres: a comparison with scintigraphy.\n\n21 years) with a multinodular goitre larger than 100 ml.\n\nIn all 20 patients signs of mechanical complications were shown on MR images.\n\nInterobserver CV was 10.1 +/-\n\n6.1%.\n\nUnderestimation of SC volumes could not be explained by the presence of cysts on the surface of the thyroid.\n\nIt is concluded that MR imaging can be used for in vivo thyroid volume estimation in large multinodular goitres.\n\nThe high precision of MR measurements makes this technique potentially useful for the evaluation of thyroid growth and non-surgical treatment for reducing goitre size.\n\nScintigraphic volume measurements do not suffice for this purpose.\n\nSafety and efficacy of RNAi therapy for transthyretin amyloidosis.\n\nThese reductions were shown to be RNAi-mediated.\n\nDose-response study on thyrotoxic patients undergoing positron emission tomography and radioiodine therapy\n\nIn contrast, patients who receive higher radiation doses (>40 Gy) at their first treatment have a high probability of control.\n\nThe probability of becoming euthyroid increases more rapidly with increasing radiation dose than the probability of becoming hypothyroid.\n\nFollowing this dose-response study, a new treatment protocol has been introduced.\n\nFurther 131I therapy will only be considered if patients are still toxic at 12 months.\n\n131-I radioiodine therapy for hyperthyroidism in patients with Graves' disease, uninodular goitre and multinodular goitre.",
